  "reason": "The records collectively suggest several relevant directions: cooling-rate control can kinetically retain an fcc phase (R4, R7), composition and thermal processing can control phase identity and fractions in Co/Fe/Ni/Ti-containing alloys (R1, R2, R5, R11, R12, R18, R20), and a two-phase microstructure can affect coercivity (R3). R8 also connects magnetic moments with fcc–hcp phase behavior. However, none discloses rapidly quenched dual-phase fcc+C14 FeCoNiTi, the compositionally matched C14-dominant comparison, phase-specific magnetic transitions, Ti-versus-Fe/Co/Ni antiferromagnetic coupling, or C14-assisted orbital/spin-canting domain-wall pinning. Thus the literature points toward phase engineering but leaves the specific intervention and magnetic mechanism as a substantial new idea."
